WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.242
Serving an LLM over an API means making model inference available to applications through a controlled request and response interface.

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:18.899
Instead of a person typing into a local chat window, application code sends structured requests to a service and receives model output back.

00:00:18.899 --> 00:00:28.555
That service may support chat completions, text completions, embeddings, structured output, streaming responses, or a mix of these patterns.

00:00:28.555 --> 00:00:32.556
LLM serving can be local, private, cloud-based, or hybrid.

00:00:32.556 --> 00:00:36.763
A local service might run on a workstation or homelab server.

00:00:36.763 --> 00:00:41.867
A private service might run inside an organization-controlled environment.

00:00:41.867 --> 00:00:46.074
A cloud service may provide managed scaling and model access.

00:00:46.074 --> 00:00:52.213
A hybrid design might route some requests to local models and others to hosted providers.

00:00:52.213 --> 00:00:58.835
Each pattern has different implications for privacy, latency, cost, reliability, and operations.

00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:04.008
Running a model interactively is not the same as operating it as a service.

00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:10.422
Interactive use is often forgiving: one user, one prompt, visible errors, and manual retries.

00:01:10.422 --> 00:01:18.147
An API service has callers, credentials, request limits, logs, monitoring, failure modes, and user expectations.

00:01:18.147 --> 00:01:27.183
It has to handle malformed input, concurrent requests, timeouts, model changes, and downstream systems that may rely on its output.

00:01:27.183 --> 00:01:34.287
API serving requires attention to performance, access control, privacy, reliability, and observability.

00:01:34.287 --> 00:01:37.667
The goal is not merely to get a model responding.

00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:55.186
The goal is to provide inference through boundaries that applications can use safely: callers are authenticated, access is scoped, data handling is understood, output is monitored, and operational behavior is predictable enough to support real workflows.
